; Leg AJ -- the BASELINE build (sixteen edits), cautious x block 3.
; THE CONTROL, and it runs CKBASE.EXE rather than CKERMITW.EXE.
;
; SS16ae leg PC is the number leg AG is being compared against, and it was
; measured in a DIFFERENT SESSION -- different day, cable re-seated, host
; possibly rebooted.  SS16ae's own closing lesson was that a control which
; is not stated is not a control; a control which is not CONTEMPORANEOUS is
; only a little better.  This leg removes that objection by reproducing PC
; on the same cable, the same afternoon, against the same fixture.
;
; It is also the only thing that can tell two different failures apart.  If
; AG comes back unchanged from PC, that means one of:
;
;   (a) edit 17 does not help at 38400, or
;   (b) something about today's bench differs from SS16ae's.
;
; Without AJ those are indistinguishable and the session produces an
; ambiguous result at the cost of a whole bench sitting.  With AJ:
;
;   AJ ~= PC (3,800 cs, rxfull 640)  ->  the bench reproduces, so AG is
;                                        a clean read either way
;   AJ != PC                         ->  something moved that is not the
;                                        edit; AG is uninterpretable and
;                                        the difference AJ-vs-AG is still
;                                        the honest measurement to quote
;
; Read exactly what AG's file says to read, in the same order, so the two
; are compared like with like.
;
; Run this leg IMMEDIATELY BEFORE OR AFTER AG, not at the other end of the
; session.  It is a control for drift, and a control taken an hour away
; from the thing it controls has drift of its own.

set line /dev/tty.usbserial-ABBFKXM1
set speed 38400
set receive timeout 20
set parity none
set prefixing cautious
set block 3
log packets s16afAJ.pkt
send rcvaj.dat
statistics
